<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zacharias Ursinus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1534">1534</a>–<a href="/years/1583">1583</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zacharias Ursinus was a German Reformed theologian, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Breslau</a> and a pupil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-melanchthon">Philip Melanchthon</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a>. Called to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> in 1561 under Elector Frederick III, he was the principal author of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg-catechism">Heidelberg Catechism</a> of 1563. After the Lutheran Elector Louis VI dismissed the Reformed teachers in 1576, he taught at Neustadt an der <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haardt">Haardt</a> until his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zacharias-ursinus.jpg" alt="Zacharias Ursinus" width="320" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zacharias_Ursinus.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
